 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Few-View Reconstruction via Regularization](12946-few-view-reconstruction-via-regularization.md)
+A family of approaches that mitigate shape-radiance ambiguity in sparse data by applying geometric or semantic priors during optimization to prevent overfitting and improve generalization to unseen views.
+
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Increases training time by the factor of the number of novel view rays relative to observed rays, as additional forward passes are required for rendering [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** No significant additional memory overhead beyond storing the novel view ray definitions and intermediate rendering buffers [G].
-*   **Performance:** Improves rendering quality and geometry accuracy, particularly in few-shot scenarios [S1]. Reduces floaters and incorrect geometry compared to training without novel view supervision [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Mitigates shape-radiance ambiguity; prevents overfitting; enhances generalization to unseen views [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires careful tuning of the novel view ray sampling strategy and loss weights [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** When training data is sparse (few-shot) and geometric accuracy is critical [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When abundant training views are available and computational resources are extremely limited [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Increases training time due to the additional rendering pass for novel views [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires memory for storing novel view ray buffers and intermediate rendering results [G].
+*   **Performance:** Improves rendering quality and geometry accuracy compared to training without novel view supervision [S1]. It significantly reduces floaters and enhances cross-scale geometric adaptation [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces overfitting, enhances geometry accuracy, and allows generalization to diverse scenes without complex scheduling [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires careful balancing of loss weights; omitting it leads to poor geometry [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Novel View Regularization is a technique employed in the FrugalNeRF framework, introduced in 2025, to enable fast convergence for extreme few-shot novel view synthesis without learned priors [S1]. It addresses the challenge of reconstructing 3D scenes from very limited input views by augmenting the training process with synthetic geometric supervision [S1].
+Novel View Regularization is a technique used in differentiable volume rendering, specifically within the context of few-shot NeRF training. It is prominently featured in the FrugalNeRF method, which aims for fast convergence in extreme few-shot novel view synthesis without learned priors [S1]. The approach leverages the idea that geometric consistency across unobserved views can serve as a strong regularizer for the implicit representation [S1].
 
